-- Runtime values, the store, and the runtime interface for the Java fragment.
--
-- WHY A SEPARATE MODULE. `JavaEval.ard` needs `RtSpec` (it is a parameter of
-- every rule) and `RtSpec` needs `Value`, so the two cannot live in one file
-- without a cycle. This module is the shared bottom layer: values, store,
-- outcomes, and the runtime interface. `JavaEval.ard` is the evaluator;
-- `RtLong.ard` is one instance of the interface.
--
-- Sources / inspiration (the survey is in the plan file):
--   * ClightBigstep.v -- `outcome` (`Out_normal | Out_break | Out_continue |
--     Out_return`) is `StmtOut` below, Java-specialised: no break, no continue,
--     plus an exception case.
--   * CompCert `Events.v` `external_call` -- the model for `RtSpec`: the
--     semantics QUANTIFIES over the runtime rather than defining it, so a
--     correctness statement does not depend on Rt's arithmetic.
--   * Owens-Myreen-Kumar-Tan, "Functional Big-Step Semantics" (ESOP 2016) --
--     the clock/fuel discipline and `Res`'s timeout case.
--   * Charguéraud, "Pretty-Big-Step Semantics" (ESOP 2013) -- the diagnosis
--     that exceptions duplicate every rule. The functional answer is `bindRes`
--     below, used everywhere instead of a propagation case per rule.

\import JavaAst
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import Set

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Store locations
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func Loc => Nat

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Values
--
-- Every value the emitted fragment can produce, and nothing else. The fragment
-- is untyped-style -- every static type is `Object` -- so this is a single flat
-- sum, not a typed hierarchy.
--
-- ARRAYS ARE HEAP REFERENCES, `Rt.Data` IS NOT. The generator mutates exactly
-- one thing: the `Rt.Fn[]` knot for `fix`, whose slots are filled after the
-- closures that read them have already captured the array. Modelling that
-- honestly requires a store. `Rt.Data` needs no indirection of its own -- it is
-- immutable and nothing in the fragment compares references -- but its `fields`
-- ARE an array, so it carries a `Loc`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data Value
  -- `Rt.BOX`. An erased proof. Identity-based in Java (`new Object()`), but
  -- nothing in the fragment tests identity, so a single atom is faithful.
  | vBox
  -- `new Rt.Data(tag, fields)` -- a constructor application.
  | vData (tag : Nat) (fields : Loc)
  -- `Object[]` and `Rt.Fn[]` alike: a reference into the store.
  | vArr Loc
  -- A closure: `new Rt.Fn(){ public Object apply(Object p){ body } }`.
  -- The environment is captured BY VALUE at creation (JLS 15.9.5, 4.12.4) --
  -- which is precisely why the `fix` knot works: what is captured is a
  -- reference to a still-empty array, and the later slot assignments are
  -- visible through it. `Env` cannot be named here (it is defined below in
  -- terms of `Value`), so it is spelled out.
  | vClos (iface : JType) (param : JIdent) (body : Array JStmt)
          (cenv : Array (\Sigma JIdent Value))
  -- A runtime constant of `Rt`, with the arguments it has collected so far.
  -- `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG` is an `Fn` that returns an `Fn`, so applying it is
  -- accumulation until saturation -- see `builtinArity`. This constructor is
  -- the arity contract whose ABSENCE was the `Rt.curry` bug (`ef2751a`).
  | vBuiltin (name : JIdent) (got : Array Value)
  -- The class `Rt` used as a receiver, i.e. `jStatic "Rt"`. Only ever a
  -- receiver for field access; it is not a first-class value in Java either.
  | vStaticRef JQIdent
  -- `Long.valueOf(5L)` -- a boxed machine integer. `Int` because it is signed.
  | vLong Int
  -- `new java.math.BigInteger("5")` -- unbounded.
  | vBigInt Int
  | vStr String
  -- A decimal `int`: a constructor tag, a field index, an array size, and the
  -- result of reading `d.tag`.
  | vInt Nat
  -- An exception OBJECT (not a thrown state): `new RuntimeException("...")`.
  -- Throwing it is a `StmtOut`, see below.
  | vExcObj (excClass : JQIdent) (msg : String)
  -- `null`. Reachable only as the initial content of a `jNewArraySized` array
  -- (Java zero-fills reference arrays), never written by the generator.
  | vNull
  -- A declared-but-unassigned local. NOT `null`: JLS 16 (definite assignment)
  -- makes reading such a local a compile-time error, so `javac` would reject
  -- the program; reading it here is `rStuck`. This is what makes "a switch
  -- branch fails to assign the result variable" a detectable mutation.
  | vUninit

\func Env => Array (\Sigma JIdent Value)

-- The store maps a `Loc` to its cells. Allocation appends, so a fresh `Loc` is
-- the old length; nothing is ever freed (the fragment has no deallocation and
-- the semantics does not model the collector).
\func Store => Array (Array Value)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Results
--
-- `rStuck` ("the semantics does not define this") is deliberately distinct from
-- `rOutOfFuel` ("the clock ran out"). The differential test needs the
-- difference: `rStuck` is a real disagreement with `java`, `rOutOfFuel` is a
-- budget that was too small. Collapsing both into `Maybe` would report the
-- second as the first.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data Res (A : \Type)
  | rOk A
  | rStuck
  | rOutOfFuel

-- The single propagation combinator. Charguéraud's duplication problem, paid
-- off once instead of per rule.
\func bindRes {A B : \Type} (r : Res A) (f : A -> Res B) : Res B \elim r
  | rOk a => f a
  | rStuck => rStuck
  | rOutOfFuel => rOutOfFuel

-- How an expression finished: with a value, or by throwing.
\data ExprOut | eVal Value | eExc Value

-- How an argument list finished: all values, or the first throw.
\data ArgsOut | aVals (Array Value) | aExc Value

-- How a statement finished (ClightBigstep's `outcome`, Java-specialised).
--
-- `oNormal` carries the environment out, because `jLocal` extends scope for the
-- statements that follow it.
--
-- THERE IS NO `oBreak`, deliberately. `JavaAst.ard:165-171`: the printer emits
-- the `break` and the block, so the fragment has no fallthrough and no break
-- node. A case body that finishes normally simply falls out of the switch --
-- which is where ClightBigstep's `outcome_switch` (`Out_break -> Out_normal`)
-- would sit, and here does nothing.
\data StmtOut | oNormal Env | oReturn Value | oExcS Value

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Array and store helpers
--
-- Spelled out rather than imported so that no import shadows String's `++`
-- (the reason `LambdaBoxEval.ard` does the same).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func nthArr {A : \Type} (n : Nat) (xs : Array A) : Maybe A \elim n, xs
  | _, nil => nothing
  | 0, x :: _ => just x
  | suc n, _ :: xs => nthArr n xs

\func snocArr {A : \Type} (xs : Array A) (y : A) : Array A \elim xs
  | nil => y :: nil
  | x :: xs => x :: snocArr xs y

-- Replace element `i`; out of range leaves the array alone (callers check).
\func setArr {A : \Type} (i : Nat) (y : A) (xs : Array A) : Array A \elim i, xs
  | _, nil => nil
  | 0, _ :: xs => y :: xs
  | suc i, x :: xs => x :: setArr i y xs

\func replicateArr {A : \Type} (n : Nat) (y : A) : Array A \elim n
  | 0 => nil
  | suc n => y :: replicateArr n y

\func natEq (m n : Nat) : Bool \elim m, n
  | 0, 0 => true
  | 0, suc _ => false
  | suc _, 0 => false
  | suc m, suc n => natEq m n

\func storeGet (st : Store) (l : Loc) : Maybe (Array Value) => nthArr l st

\func storeAlloc (st : Store) (cells : Array Value) : \Sigma Store Loc =>
  (snocArr st cells, st.len)

-- Write one cell. `nothing` when the location or the index is out of range --
-- the caller turns that into `rStuck` or an exception, as appropriate.
\func storeSet (st : Store) (l : Loc) (i : Nat) (v : Value) : Maybe Store =>
  \case storeGet st l \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just cells => \case nthArr i cells \with {
      | nothing => nothing
      | just _ => just (setArr l (setArr i v cells) st)
    }
  }

\func lookupVar (env : Env) (x : JIdent) : Maybe Value \elim env
  | nil => nothing
  | e :: env => \case e.1 == x \with {
    | true => just e.2
    | false => lookupVar env x
  }

-- Rebinding shadows by prepending; `jAssign` to a local uses this, and lookup
-- scans from the head, so the newest binding wins.
\func bindVar (env : Env) (x : JIdent) (v : Value) : Env => (x, v) :: env

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The runtime interface
--
-- `runtime/Rt.java` is hand-written Java: 618 lines using interfaces, nested
-- classes, instance fields, `instanceof`, loops, real arithmetic, generics and
-- `Thread` -- none of which is in this fragment. It is therefore NOT modelled.
-- It is a PARAMETER, and this class is its contract.
--
-- That is CompCert's treatment of `external_call` (`Events.v`) and
-- Malfunction's treatment of OCaml primitives. Two consequences worth stating:
--
--   * the correctness theorem quantifies over `RtSpec`, so it does not depend
--     on Rt's arithmetic being right -- Rt's obligations become an explicit
--     hypothesis instead of a silent assumption;
--   * writing `builtinArity` down IS the fix for the `Rt.curry` class of bug.
--     That bug (`ef2751a`) was a collector advertising an arity it did not need,
--     and it was silent precisely because no arity contract existed anywhere.
--
-- What is NOT covered by an instance stays `nothing`, hence `rStuck` -- a named
-- gap rather than a wrong answer.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\class RtSpec
  -- The value of the field `Rt.<name>`: `BOX`, `PRIM_ADD_LONG`, `TRUE`, ...
  | rtField : JIdent -> Maybe Value
  -- How many arguments `Rt.<name>` must collect before it computes.
  | builtinArity : JIdent -> Maybe Nat
  -- Run a saturated builtin. The argument array has length `builtinArity name`.
  --
  -- THE STORE IS THREADED THROUGH, and this is not incidental. A comparison
  -- primitive returns `Rt.TRUE`/`Rt.FALSE`, i.e. `Data(1, {})`/`Data(0, {})`,
  -- and a `Data`'s fields live in the store -- so a builtin that returns a
  -- constructor must ALLOCATE. An earlier version of this field had type
  -- `JIdent -> Array Value -> Res Value`, which made every such primitive
  -- inexpressible: `PRIM_EQB`, `PRIM_BLE`, `PRIM_BLT` and the whole `DEC_*`
  -- decidable family. That was found by running `ExampleMatMul.matMulProgram`,
  -- which declares `prim_eqb_int` as an axiom, and getting `rStuck`.
  | builtinRun : Store -> JIdent -> Array Value -> Res (\Sigma Store Value)
